{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the design note on a Git read token after its key is revoked, at head 98d621c1, for request 3db806df and review request c78bb459. Verdict: APPROVED, as a design with no code built. The practice section is what makes one hour believable, and the note keeps what the pages say apart from what nobody has run. It may land. Four points below belong in the build's request, and none needs the note to change first.\n\n## Your five questions\n\n1. **The ID: the sources support it, and keeping it is right as a design. Run real call 1 before the build keeps a single ID.**\n   - I fetched the types at the version you cite. `revokeToken(tokenOrId)` says \"Plaintext token or token ID\", and it returns true or false as you quote.\n   - The REST page's example does show an `id` of `0123456789abcdef` beside the token `art_v1_0123456789abcdef…`. Those look like placeholder digits, so this is weak evidence. If it were real, 64 of 160 bits would sit in custody, and the rest would still be strong.\n   - The larger reason for the call is the one you name: `hold@2` already writes a host ID into an entry, and a member can read that entry. So call 1 matters whatever the planner decides here. I would run it first of the three.\n2. **`revoke-reads`: a handled mark makes a repeat safe against a second host call. It does not keep Mara's sentence whole, and the total does not change the design.**\n   - **The repeat.** `revokeToken` on a token already revoked returns false, which lands in `gone`, so a repeat harms nothing. Mark a row only after an answer, and leave a thrown call unmarked so that it counts in `left`. The note implies this, and the build should state it.\n   - **The counts.** The evidence counts what one attempt did. After a lost reply, attempt 1's revocations are in no entry, and attempt 2 can read `revoked: 0, left: 0` and `confirmed`. That is true and misleads the reader who is told \"the host confirmed one\". Have each row keep its result, and report the totals across attempts.\n   - **The rule of one request for one attempt.** `packages/git/src/host.ts` states it for host calls that make something. These calls only stop something, and each is safe to repeat, so I accept the exception. Say so at that comment when it is built.\n   - **The reservation.** By `held.ts`, `one(k)` is `n(k)` times the outcome entry and what it starts, and `n` is 6 for 3 attempts with no mark that opens anything. So 16 operations reserve 96 entries, in the amounts where a static entry size stands in for bytes, which at 256 KiB each is about 24 MiB. I did not find the validator's limit, so I cannot say that it passes. If it fails, the count falls to something like the 6 that `revoke` holds. That changes a number and no part of the design. The first build step should run the validator on the data.\n3. **The lane: yes, leave it.** The route gives one fork for about 15 minutes, the token can read only what the fork holds, and `stage` takes only a signed step. The bound is short and known, and a notice to every lane is a larger change than that exposure earns. A reader needs one thing stated, and you state it: the fork can hold the room's default branch as of its seed, so a thief reads those commits too. They were readable by that key before the revocation anyway.\n4. **The correction: the note is right, and the sentence was mine to catch.** The landed sentence says \"the provider's `revokeToken` takes the plaintext\". The code's own type at `artifacts-host.ts:31` says \"A token's plaintext or its ID\", and `mintRead` reads `expiresAt` and `plaintext` and drops `id`. I approved that sentence in a3bb25e2 without reading the type. The conclusion holds today, that the service cannot revoke a taken token, and the reason on Artifacts is the dropped ID. An implementation note records its own head, so correcting it here is the right form.\n5. **The table: each row holds, with two words to sharpen.**\n   - **The 600 seconds.** A token the key holds was minted no later than its take, and the take comes at most 600 seconds after the revocation, so expiry is at most lifetime plus 600. That is the argument from my review of the trace, and it holds on both pins.\n   - **The `retired` row.** \"The token's expiry\" is true and gives no number. No notice goes out, so nothing refuses the session either: the bound is the pin's lifetime plus 600 seconds, as in the rows above it. Give that number.\n   - **The scene.** \"Commits that we publish after 09:41 are safe from that token\" is stronger than the table's row, which says that no page gives the host's delay. Real call 1 records the delay. Until then, let the scene say \"after the host applies it\".\n\n## Other points\n\n- **One hour.** I agree. Your strongest reason is the third: it removes 23 hours from the worst case whether or not `revoke-reads` runs. The cost is real only if a host cuts a reply at expiry, and real calls 2 and 3 settle that. So I would not build the lifetime change before those calls have run, and a 10 GB older repository is outside the new pin anyway.\n- **The new pin.** It adds to the same lists that `separate-recovery` grew, and it comes after that cohort and the founder design's pin. The planner orders those.\n- **Rita's REST step.** The docs step can land before any code, as you recommend. It should say that nobody has run it, as the note does, until someone has.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n**Architecture.** Sound. Host ID in custody, plaintext dropped, and the token's end bound by the pin is the smallest design that gives a true sentence on both hosts. Your table of what custody of a plaintext would mean is the part I would keep for the next person who asks.\n\n**Security.** The design narrows the worst case from 24 hours to one hour on new rooms, and on Artifacts it can end a token early. It claims nothing about what the thief already took. Older rooms keep 24 hours, and the note says so.\n\n**Simplification.** Acceptable. `revoke-reads` is the one new mechanism, and you make it optional behind the lifetime change.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- **Sources.** I fetched the types at `@cloudflare/workers-types@5.20261101.1` and read `revokeToken` and the `createToken` lines near it, and I fetched the REST page and read its example. I did not read the other host pages that the note quotes, so those quotations rest on the note.\n- **Code.** `artifacts-host.ts`'s type and `mintRead`, the head of `held.ts`, `branch.holds` and the destination's `read-token` and `compromised` in `destination.ts`, and the landed sentence. I did not trace `hold.ts`, `workspace-host.ts` or `credential-store.ts`, so the section on the workspace token and the custody table rest on the note and on what I read in earlier reviews.\n- **At this head.** It adds one file against main 273cbd75, and the worktree stayed clean. I ran no test, because no code changes.\n- **Not done.** I used no independent reader, and I made no call to any host beyond those two public pages.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c5dc0d952f38de417d02b31442748ba14f9b68ba","binding":"assigned","head":"98d621c1306a5ae02bad50b8e5dcef4abcc01fd3","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8b03b1e1a1bbecc1aa386cba470422798eab6540\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a11a737a5badd3639e8beb6ac2ce2dbbaa59f843\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:090ac51acb36f804632c884381734ccb122b00c3","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}